# DHealth Native — the deployment contract (DHEALTH-NATIVE-DEPLOY-001).
#
#     cp deploy/native/native.env.example .env.native
#     # fill in every REQUIRED value below
#     docker compose -f docker-compose.native.yml --env-file .env.native up -d --wait
#
# Deliberately a separate file from `.env.example`. That one is the historical
# maternity/reference-connector deployment's contract and asks for roughly forty OpenMRS,
# OpenELIS, MariaDB and certificate variables that a Native deployment has no use for. Somebody
# deploying only DHealth Native should not have to work out which of those matter; here, all of
# them do.
#
# **Every REQUIRED value below fails the deployment closed when absent.** Compose refuses to start
# with `set <NAME>` rather than selecting a default, which is what stops a box coming up on a
# guessable password nobody chose. The OPTIONAL values have defaults that are safe to keep.
#
# Never commit a filled-in copy. `.env.native` is gitignored.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED — the deployment will refuse to start without these
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The domain the three public vhosts hang off: app.<BASE_DOMAIN>, id.<BASE_DOMAIN>,
# exchange.<BASE_DOMAIN>. DNS for all three must point at this host before Caddy can obtain
# publicly trusted certificates. `localhost` works for a local qualification and gives Caddy's
# internal CA instead -- fine for proving routing, never a claim about public issuance.
BASE_DOMAIN=

# The origin a BROWSER reaches Keycloak on, including the scheme. Keycloak builds every issuer,
# every redirect and every login form action from it. Point it at the public id. vhost --
# https://id.<BASE_DOMAIN> -- never at an internal service name: DHEALTH-NATIVE-MVP-1.0 found that
# an internal value makes the login form post credentials to a hostname only the container network
# can resolve, so nobody can sign in at all.
KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL=

# PostgreSQL. Three separate credentials on purpose: the superuser creates and hardens the two
# tenant databases, and neither tenant may reach the other's (see deploy/native/db-init/).
POSTGRES_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=
KEYCLOAK_DB_PASSWORD=
ADAPTER_DB_PASSWORD=

# The Keycloak administrator. This account can mint credentials for the whole realm; treat it as
# the most sensitive value in this file.
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# Resolved into the committed realm JSON at import time, which is how the realm file stays free of
# secrets. The confidential client the Exchange API audience is minted for.
KEYCLOAK_EXCHANGE_API_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL — defaults are production-safe; change only with a reason
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

TZ=Africa/Djibouti
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER=admin
KEYCLOAK_DB_NAME=keycloak
KEYCLOAK_DB_USER=keycloak
ADAPTER_DB_NAME=dhealth_exchange
ADAPTER_DB_USER=adapter

# Outbound retry policy for the Exchange's own queues.
ADAPTER_RETRY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
ADAPTER_RETRY_MULTIPLIER_SECONDS=1
ADAPTER_RETRY_MAX_SECONDS=30

# Host ports Caddy publishes. Change only when something else already owns 80/443.
CADDY_HTTP_PORT=80
CADDY_HTTPS_PORT=443

# Overrides for the OIDC contract. The defaults derive correctly from KEYCLOAK_PUBLIC_URL: the
# issuer is the public origin (that is what a real token's `iss` carries) and the JWKS URL is the
# internal one (that is what the adapter can reach). Set these only if your Keycloak is somewhere
# this deployment does not run it.
# EXCHANGE_OIDC_ISSUER=
# EXCHANGE_OIDC_JWKS_URL=
# EXCHANGE_OIDC_AUDIENCE=dhealth-exchange-api

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BACKUP AND RECOVERY (DHEALTH-NATIVE-RECOVERY-001 / ADR-062)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Where encrypted archives are written. A bind mount, never a named volume: an archive stored in a
# volume of the project it protects dies with that project. **Point this at separate storage.** The
# default keeps a first run working and is not a recovery plan.
# NATIVE_BACKUP_DIR=./backup/native-archives

# Off-site is REQUIRED by default, and Native has no uploader yet -- so an unconfigured run ends
# FAILED with its encrypted local archive retained. That is the honest state of an unbuilt feature,
# not a bug. Set it to false only for an explicit local verification, and do not read a run that
# needed it as off-site qualified. Setting BACKUP_S3_BUCKET produces an explicit failure saying so,
# rather than letting anyone believe a copy left the host.
# BACKUP_REQUIRE_OFFSITE=true

# NOT here, and never: BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_PASSPHRASE.
#
# The passphrase is supplied by the operator at run time, from their own secret custody:
#
#     read -rs BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_PASSPHRASE && export BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_PASSPHRASE
#     scripts/native/backup.sh
#
# It is in no env file and no Compose file (`adapter/tests/test_native_recovery_tooling.py` asserts
# that), never on a command line, and never in the archive. ADR-025: a passphrase whose only copy
# was on the host that was destroyed is not a disaster-recovery passphrase -- keep a recoverable
# copy somewhere else. Runbook: `docs/NATIVE-RECOVERY.md`.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT here, on purpose
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Demo values (DEMO_CLINICIAN_*, DEMO_LAB_TECH_*, DEMO_RELEASER_*, ADAPTER_DEMO_ENVIRONMENT) live
# in `demo/native/demo.env` and belong only to the demo overlay. ADAPTER_DEMO_ENVIRONMENT=1 is one
# of the three independent facts `demo_seed.py` requires before it will clear a single row; a
# production deployment must never set it, and this file never mentions it again.
#
# OpenMRS, OpenELIS, MariaDB and certificate variables (OMRS_*, OE_*, DAHER_OPENELIS_SITE_CODE)
# belong to the preserved reference/maternity deployment and are in `.env.example`. Native needs
# none of them.
#
# WhatsApp/Meta notification variables (META_*) are only needed by a deployment that switches
# notifications on, and ADR-003's gate has not been cleared: see `docs/META-WHATSAPP-RUNBOOK.md`.
# Without them the notification surface fails closed, which is the correct state for a deployment
# that has no WhatsApp Business Account.
#
# DHEALTH-NATIVE-RECOVERY-001 note: `docker-compose.native.yml` passes the notification contract
# through as of 2026-09-25 -- before that it passed none of it, so the feature was unreachable in
# this deployment and the Meta webhook route at the edge could only answer 403. It is OFF by
# default (`DHEALTH_NOTIFICATIONS_ENABLED=false`) and every value is optional in that state. Turn it
# on and a missing credential becomes a refusal to start, naming the variable -- deliberately, so a
# half-configured deployment cannot look healthy while silently sending nothing.
# DHEALTH_PATIENT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL defaults to https://app.<BASE_DOMAIN>, which is correct for
# Native.
